---
import WorksheetLayout from '../../../../layouts/WorksheetLayout.astro';
---
<WorksheetLayout
  title="Client Brief"
  lesson="/courses/mfedu-2026/design-for-someone-else/"
  intro="Design for someone who isn't you. Read your client story, then work through the brief like a pro studio."
>
  <h2>My client</h2>
  <p>Who am I designing for, and what do they need the world to know?</p>
  <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div><div></div></div>

  <h2>What matters to them</h2>
  <p>List three things your client cares about that should show up in the design.</p>
  <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div><div></div></div>

  <h2>Element decisions</h2>
  <div class="ws-grid">
    <div>
      <p><strong>Color</strong> — and why:</p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div></div>
      <p><strong>Line</strong> — and why:</p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><strong>Shape</strong> — and why:</p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div></div>
      <p><strong>Texture</strong> — and why:</p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div><div></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="ws-hint">"Pink is my favorite color" is not a reason — "pink reads friendly, and the shop wants new customers to feel welcome" is.</p>

  <h2>Where the light goes</h2>
  <p>Sketch the outfit. Mark where lights would go and what they're saying — do they glow, blink, spell, or point?</p>
  <div class="ws-box ws-box--tall"></div>

  <h2>Present it</h2>
  <ul class="ws-checks">
    <li>I explained each element choice with a reason from the client's story</li>
    <li>Someone else could look at my sketch and guess the client</li>
  </ul>
</WorksheetLayout>
